THIRD-PARTY ATTRIBUTION: `aicomp_sdk/`, `aicomp_sdk_exfil_v2_1/`, and
`aicomp_sdk_exfil_v2_2/` in this repository are derived from or extend the
official SDK/test environment of the "AI Agent Security: Multi-Step Tool
Attacks" Kaggle competition (OpenAI, Google, and IEEE, 2026), which the
competition's own Official Rules (Sections 1.7, 2.4.a) publish under the
MIT License. Original attribution:

  Manish Bhatt, Catherine Huang, Owen Vallis, Jess Chang, Sherin Mathews,
  Blake Gatto, Maria Cruz, Yao Yan, and Martyna Plomecka. AI Agent Security
  - Multi-Step Tool Attacks. https://kaggle.com/competitions/
  ai-agent-security-multi-step-tool-attacks, 2026. Kaggle.

---
PROVENANCE NOTE (confirmed against the competition's own Official Rules,
not assumed):

  - Rule 1.6 ("WINNER LICENSE TYPE"): MIT 2.0.
  - Rule 1.7 ("DATA ACCESS AND USE"): "The test environment is published
    under MIT 2.0."
  - Rule 2.4.a ("COMPETITION DATA"): "The Competition Data is also subject
    to the following terms and conditions: MIT License," and may be used
    "for any purpose, whether commercial or non-commercial, including...
    for academic research and education."

  MIT was therefore chosen because it is the SAME license the competition
  organizers (OpenAI, Google, IEEE) already use for the underlying SDK/test
  environment this repository builds on (`aicomp_sdk/`,
  `aicomp_sdk_exfil_v2_1/`, `aicomp_sdk_exfil_v2_2/`) -- not merely a
  convenient default.

  ONE REMAINING NUANCE, flagged rather than silently resolved: Rule 3.6.b
  ("Public Code Sharing") states that Competition Code shared publicly
  "is required to share it on Kaggle.com on the discussion forum or
  notebooks associated specifically with the Competition for the benefit
  of all competitors." This appears to be a VENUE requirement (where you
  post it), separate from the licensing question this note otherwise
  resolves. To be safely compliant with the letter of that clause, in
  addition to this GitHub repository, also post a link to it (or the
  working note itself) on the competition's own Kaggle discussion forum /
  notebook, rather than relying on GitHub alone.
